  "articleSummary": "The article argues that agentic cyber threats emerge not from a single breakthrough but from multiplying two improving capabilities—agents’ autonomous task duration and orchestrators’ ability to coordinate many sub-agents—while stolen access shifts compute costs onto victims. It cites METR’s task-horizon research, Claude Code’s reported role in the GTG-1002 espionage campaign, and “LLMjacking” through stolen cloud credentials to illustrate endurance, breadth, and attacker economics. Its probability examples show how many cheap attempts can create substantial aggregate risk even when individual success rates are low, overwhelming human-paced security review. A pointed, darkly humorous security analysis aimed at skeptical engineering and security teams, it repeatedly uses Terminator/Skynet imagery and billing metaphors to frame the threat as persistent, distributed automation running on someone else’s tab.",
